{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_20261026060741690370a0c4314adf","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still 429. It's been ~4-5 minutes since the agent's last attempt. The quota seems very tight (maybe 1 request per hour, or the account is out of quota).\n\nLet me try one final spacing strategy: wait a longer period by spawning a background subagent that runs for a few minutes doing nothing harmful... Actually a background agent that just sleeps? They can't sleep either (Bash denied). A subagent could do a \"thinking\" loop — not real wall time necessarily.\n\nHmm. Actually a background agent runs and I get notified when done. If I spawn an agent with instructions to retry the web_reader every attempt until success, up to ~30 attempts, that's ~5+ minutes of wall time, and it could get through if the quota resets on a per-minute basis. The previous agent did 46 attempts over 3 minutes without success, so per-minute reset is ruled out.\n\nGiven evidence: the limiter allowed ~1-2 requests at session start, then nothing for 10+ minutes. This looks like an exhausted quota (daily/hourly).\n\nI should now honestly report. But the task demands a list of 8-20 inscriptions with references. Without web access, I can only provide what I know with confidence labels — but the instruction explicitly says don't invent numbers; if I can't verify, say so honestly.\n\nI do have some genuinely well-known, verifiable-from-memory items, but the risk of wrong numbers is high. The user explicitly said: \"Ako nešto ne možeš da potvrdiš ... reci to iskreno — ne izmišljaj brojeve.\" So the correct behavior: deliver the framework (corpora, what is known about coverage), flag that I could not verify any numbers online due to access failure, and provide only those items I'm reasonably confident about with low confidence marking, clearly separated from verified data. Better: give NO fabricated specifics; instead list the corpora and the known anchor points that are extremely well established:\n\n- CIL III: Naissus inscriptions are in CIL III p. 232, 1030 and CIL III Suppl. numbers around 8197ff — this the user themselves provided; I can't verify beyond that.\n- IM: \"Inscriptions de la Mésie Supérieure\" — volumes: IM I (Viminacium and Scupi?), actually the series is: Vol I: Viminacium et Scupi (1979?), Vol II: Viminiacium? Let me recall correctly: \n  - IM I: M. Mirković, \"Inscriptions de la Mésie Supérieure. I. Viminacium et Scupi\" (Beograd 1979) — actually IM I is \"Viminacium et Scupi\" by M. Mirković? Hmm, some say IM I = Viminacium (Mirković), IM II = Viminacium? Let me recall the standard series \"Inschriften aus Moesia Superior\" / \"Inscriptions de la Mesie Superieure\" (IMS), published in Belgrade:\n    - IMS I: Viminacium et Scupi (M. Mirković, 1979)? \n    - IMS II: Aquae / Ptolemais? \n    - IMS III/IV: Margum, Naissus? \n  Actually the standard: IMS IV = Margum (P. Petrović), IMS V = Aquae? IMS VI = Scupi (Dušanić)? I'm not sure. \n  I recall IMS VI = Naissus? Hmm. There's a known reference \"IMS VI, 33\" for Naissus? Honestly uncertain.\n  \n  Widely cited: \"IMS 2\" no... I recall AE 1988, 985 = ... no.\n\nI should not guess. The honest thing is to report the access failure and give a verified-base report of what could not be confirmed, plus a concrete plan (EDC POST form, EDH bulk CSV, Trismegistos, Last Statues of Antiquity).\n\nWait — before concluding, maybe I should try one more spaced attempt; total elapsed is now maybe 8-10 min since first success. One more single attempt, then finalize.","signature":"14976c4a79d74b86a45344c0"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"call_b0d4e2e5af4a4f86b1e2d808","session_id":"a06178bc-e4e6-4413-967f-cf91359c7b1b","uuid":"280e08e6-5a51-4c23-a1cc-cc0154201f52","timestamp":"2026-10-25T22:08:04.615Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","t