{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202610260612105574d7b079c0466c","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Critical situation: Agent A reports that WebSearch/WebFetch/Bash are all permission-denied for subagents too, and the only permitted web tool (mcp web_reader) hit a hard rate limit for ~70 calls over 15 minutes. Only 2 calls got through the limiter: EDCS search page (unreadable) and EDH API (Anubis anti-bot).\n\nThis changes everything: the web research thread is largely blocked. The remaining 3 agents (B, C, D) will likely return similar failures.\n\nKey data points:\n1. The web_reader MCP tool worked at least twice (2 calls passed the limiter). The rate limit is provider-side (ZAI, code 1302) and may recover over time.\n2. EDH API is behind Anubis anti-bot even when reachable.\n3. No agent can verify any web number today.\n\nOptions:\n- (1) Wait for the rate limit to cool and retry web_reader through agents, slowly (one call every N minutes). Unknown recovery time; agents B/C/D are still running and will burn quota.\n- (2) Produce the deliverables honestly with what IS available: local database + the monograph quotes captured pre-crash + my (the model's) general knowledge, all marked with explicit low confidence for unverified-from-web claims, and record the access limitation honestly as required (\"ograničenja upisati iskreno\").\n- (3) Keep retrying web_reader via an agent with slow pacing.\n\nThe task rules say: \"ogranicenja upisati iskreno\" (record limitations honestly) — this suggests the deliverable must exist even if imperfect. The best path: \n- Let the remaining agents finish (they might get a few successful web_reader calls — each call that passes gives real data).\n- Then resume agent E (or a fresh slow agent) with pacing instructions to try web_reader once, wait, try again — targeting the highest-value URLs (LSA for Mediana statues; EDCS; maybe Wikipedia/basic pages that could carry inscription transcriptions).\n- Meanwhile, I write the deliverables from local anchors + general knowledge, clearly marking poverenje: nisko/srednje, and never inventing Greek text: I must NOT fabricate the Greek inscription text. For the Roimetalkos inscriptions, what do I actually know from my general knowledge? I know the general scholarly description (porphyry Asclepius and Hygieia from Mediana with Greek dedications by a person named Rhoimetalkes (Romanized Thracian name) and honorific \"διασημότατος\"; dedicated together with his wife). I do NOT have a verified full Greek transcription in memory. I must mark that as unavailable and give the fragments attested in the local monograph quotes only.\n\nAlso the DB hint: \"porfirne statue sa natpisima traziti u IM za baze\" — the base inscriptions likely published in IMS (Inscriptions de la Mésie Supérieure). I recall IMS volume for Naissus... I'm not sure of exact numbers — don't invent.\n\nActually, wait — one more angle: my own general knowledge of the actual known facts. Let me carefully recall what is well-established scholarship about Mediana inscriptions (to be marked \"srednje/nisko — opšte znanje, nije web-verifikovano danas\"):\n\n1. CIL III: Naissus inscriptions — CIL III 8197-8218 or so region? I genuinely am not certain about individual numbers. CIL III 8197 — I shouldn't assert. The ZADATAK says \"CIL III 8197ff\" as the given range (the user's own prompt asserts it). I can cite that as \"prema zadatku/bazi\" with srednje poverenje, noting not verified.\n\n2. The famous Naissus inscriptions include:\n- Dedication to Jupiter by veterans, milestones (from the Naissus road network), the sarcophagus of a physician... Actually well-known: \"CIL III 8197\" might be... I don't know. Honest approach: don't list specific numbers except those verifiable.\n\n3. IMS: The series \"Inscriptions de la Mésie Supérieure\" (Sigrid & Belgrade, French titles). Volumes: IMS I (Viminacium, M. Mirković 1986), IMS II (Aquae?/Horreum Margi, PETROVIĆ P.), IMS III (Timacum Minus?), IMS IV (Naissus — P. Petrović, \"Inscriptions de la Mésie Supérieure, vol. IV: Naissus/Remiana\"? I believe IMS IV = P. Petrović, Inscriptions de la Mésie supérieure. IV. Naissus, Remesiana, ... 1979/1986?), IMS V/VI... I recall: IMS IV: Naissus (Petar Petrović 1979). This is moderately standard knowledge — mark srednje (neprovereno danas). Hmm, actually I recall IMS volumes: I Viminacium (Mirković), II Horreum Margi (Petrović P.), III Timacum Minus (Petrović V.P.?), IV Naissus - Remesiana? (P. Petrović), VI Scupi? (Sasel Kos?)... IMS VI is Scupi by Julijana Sasel. Not sure. I'll state IMS IV for Naissus with poverenje nisko-srednje and flag.\n\n4. Rhoimetalkes: Thracian royal name (Rhoemetalces I-III, kings of Odrysian Thrace, 1st c. BC - 3rd c. AD; also a son of Cotys). Etymology: Thracian, possibly from *rhoime- (\"rock/crag\"? Detschew discusses; meaning debated). The name in the Mediana dedication is a Thracian name borne by a Roman-era aristocrat with the honorific διασημότατος (\"most illustrious\") — high imperial/late antique honorific. The dedicator could be a Thracian/proto-Bulgarian era magnate? Actually \"diasemotatos\" (διασημότατος) is a late Roman senatorial honorific, used 4th c. — consistent with a 4th-century dedicator. Whether he was related to the Thracian royal house is speculation — flag it.\n\nAlso the ZADATAK hints \"Roimetalkos (Moimetalkos?)\" — the base's reading might be uncertain (Ροιμητάλκης vs Μοιμητάλκης). And \"mogući Telesfor\" — a confusion in the DB between the group Asclepius+Telesphoros and the dedicator. I'll record as konflikt/nejasnoća.\n\n5. Dea Dardanica: The statue from Mediana entrance — published by M. Vasić? I recall \"Dea Dardanica\" statue from Mediana is in the Narodni muzej Niš. There are epigraphic attestations of \"Dardanicae\"? Hmm. In CIL there are dedications to \"Dardania\"? Not sure. Flag as unverified.\n\nHonesty rules: I must not present unverified specifics as verified. The deliverable will have:\n- What the local base/monograph says (visoko poverenje — citat iz monografije sa linijama)\n- What is general knowledge (nisko/srednje, explicitly marked \"nije web-verifikovano danas\")\n- What couldn't be verified (EDCS/EDH/EDH/LSA unosi)\n\nBut before settling for that, give the web one more chance: the remaining 3 agents (B, C, D) are still running — maybe they got through occasionally. And I should resume agent A and E with a slow-pacing strategy later.\n\nActually agent A already spent 15 min and ~70 calls. The other three are presumably hitting the same wall but may catch occasional successful calls. Let me wait for their notifications before deciding. Meanwhile, I can start drafting nalaz.md's locally-grounded sections (w-4 catalog with monograph quotes, section structure, 3D section from DB knowledge).\n\nOne thing I can also do: check whether the DB files (baza_podataka.json etc.) are readable — they're also outside my dir (blocked). But SINTEZA.md? Also outside. Only my folder is writable/readable now. OK.\n\nSo: draft nalaz.md skeleton now in my folder? Writing a partial file then rewriting is fine — it's my own file. But maybe better to wait for the final agent reports to write once. I'll draft content in my head and wait.\n\nActually, let me use this waiting time productively: prepare the exact quotes table from the pre-crash greps (they're in conversation context — fine, no work needed).\n\nI'll wait for the next notifications. No new tool calls needed right now... Actually, one useful action: schedule nothing; just end turn and wait for B/C/D notifications.\n\nHmm, but there's risk B/C/D burn all remaining quota quickly and return empty. Then I resume them one at a time with 1-call-then-wait pacing. Fine — sequential recovery.\n\nLet me end turn with brief status.","signature":"6127181014204f978ddd0ff8"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a06178bc-e4e6-4413-967f-cf91359c7b1b","uuid":"8072aaf3-f635-4c60-a538-1e522dcd87ca","timestamp":"2026-10-25T22:13:02.766Z"}
