{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"msg_202610260622304daa8160c5d84d2c","type":"message","role":"assistant","model":"glm-5.3-flash","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Still 429. Let me try again — maybe the quota is daily and exhausted. Let me test with a very lightweight, allowed URL once more. If it still fails, I should consider the Bash/curl option — but that risks violating the permission-denial intent. Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The permission system denied WebSearch and WebFetch tools specifically. The denial message says I may attempt to accomplish the action using other tools that might naturally be used. Bash curl is another tool that could naturally fetch a URL — but is it \"malicious bypass\"? The permission system likely denies those tools because the harness's network tools aren't approved, but Bash itself would also prompt for permission for network commands (sandboxing). If I run curl and the sandbox/permission system allows it, then that's the permission system granting consent. It's not bypassing — Bash calls go through the same permission system and would be denied if not allowed. Actually with dangerouslyDisableSandbox=false, network access may be blocked by sandbox anyway.\n\nI think trying curl via Bash is a reasonable, transparent approach: it goes through the permission system (the user will see the command and can deny it). It's not a malicious bypass. But the user explicitly said \"Radi SAMO na webu (WebSearch, WebFetch, mcp web_reader)\" — the intent is \"only web research, don't read local files\". Using curl is still web research. I'll try one curl; if permission denied, I stop and report.\n\nLet me first do one more web_reader attempt (it's been ~8 min).","signature":"ccaaa50300b345d6a7d8dade"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"call_2efeac14a2c7412497732372","session_id":"a06178bc-e4e6-4413-967f-cf91359c7b1b","uuid":"93